   "o-bipa-breach": {
    "type": "outcome",
    "tier": "required",
    "title": "Biometric breach: notify under IL PIPA Sec. 10 — and assess BIPA Sec. 15/20 liability exposure",
    "summary": "BIPA has no breach-notification provision — the notification duty for breached Illinois biometric data flows from IL PIPA Sec. 10 (biometric data is within PIPA 'personal information'). BIPA Sec. 15(e) requires biometric data to be stored, transmitted, and protected using the reasonable standard of care within the entity's industry, and at the same or higher level than other confidential and sensitive info. A breach puts Sec. 15(e) compliance in issue — assess whether that standard was met; a breach alone does not establish a violation, and Sec. 20 liability additionally requires a negligent, intentional, or reckless violation. BIPA Sec. 20 provides liquidated damages of $1,000 per negligent violation or $5,000 per intentional/reckless violation, or actual damages, whichever is greater — and such damages are discretionary, not automatic (Cothron v White Castle). 9th Circuit's Patel v Facebook confirmed that BIPA notice-and-consent violations create concrete Article III injury independent of pecuniary loss ($650M settlement). Illinois Supreme Court (Cothron, 2023) held each scan is a separate violation; the 2024 legislative amendment reduced this to per-person aggregate liability for some claims.",
    "actions": [
     "Run the IL PIPA Sec. 10 notification analysis now (the Illinois-residents branch of this wizard) — the notification duty comes from PIPA, not BIPA — and run NY/CA/other-state breach analyses in parallel as applicable",
     "Document Sec. 15(a) written retention policy and confirm destruction schedule was followed",
     "Confirm Sec. 15(b) written notice + written release was obtained from each affected subject before collection",
     "Sec. 15(e): assess whether the industry standard of care and same-or-more-protective handling were met — document the security program; do not concede a violation merely because a breach occurred",
     "Anticipate Sec. 20 private actions — preserve all collection-consent and retention-policy records",
     "Patel v Facebook makes federal-court standing easy for BIPA plaintiffs — assume class certification is likely"
    ],
    "cites": [
     "il-bipa-15",
     "il-bipa-20",
     "il-pipa-10",
     "case-patel-facebook",
     "case-cothron-white-castle"
    ]
   }
