   "o-li": {
    "type": "outcome",
    "tier": "warn",
    "title": "Art. 6(1)(f) — Legitimate interests",
    "summary": "Subjects have a right to object under Art. 21(1). You must stop processing unless you can demonstrate compelling legitimate grounds that override the subject's rights, or for legal claims. Direct-marketing objections under Art. 21(2) are absolute — no balancing. Meta Platforms (C-252/21) tightened the LI balancing for online platforms — for personalized advertising in a dominant-platform context, LI will rarely hold. CJEU C-40/17 Fashion ID requires each joint controller to independently pursue an LI. Both are EU authority — persuasive only in the UK post-Brexit. UK divergence: the Data (Use and Access) Act 2025 (DUAA 2025) adds 'recognised legitimate interests' (UK GDPR Art. 6(1)(ea) + Annex — e.g. national security, emergencies, crime, safeguarding), which need no balancing test. It also names direct marketing, intra-group administrative transmission, and network security as purposes that may constitute legitimate interests. Outside the recognized list the three-part LIA remains the UK default. Commencement is phased — verify in-force status and current ICO guidance.",
    "actions": [
     "Conduct and document a three-part LIA: (1) legitimate interest, (2) necessity, (3) balancing — for EU processing follow the EDPB Guidelines 1/2024 methodology (verify final adopted version); CJEU C-621/22 KNLTB confirms purely commercial interests can qualify",
     "UK: check first whether the purpose is a DUAA 2025 'recognised legitimate interest' (UK GDPR Art. 6(1)(ea) + Annex) — if so, no balancing test is required; otherwise run the standard LIA",
     "Disclose the LI in your privacy notice (Art. 13(1)(d) / 14(2)(b))",
     "Direct marketing: subject's Art. 21(2) objection is absolute; stop immediately — this holds in both regimes, including where UK direct marketing rests on LI",
     "Children's data — LI balancing tilts strongly against the controller (Art. 6(1)(f) flags 'in particular where the data subject is a child')",
     "Public authorities cannot use Art. 6(1)(f) for their public-task processing — final subparagraph of Art. 6(1)",
     "Art. 17(1)(c) erasure: required if no overriding legitimate grounds",
     "For embedded plugins / joint controllership: each party must have its own LI (Fashion ID (C-40/17) — EU authority; ICO guidance takes a similar line)"
    ],
    "cites": [
     "gdpr-art-6",
     "gdpr-art-17",
     "gdpr-art-21",
     "case-meta-bka",
     "case-fashion-id",
     "uk-duaa-2025",
     "guide-edpb-1-2024",
     "case-knltb",
     "guide-ico-lawful-basis"
    ]
   },
   "o-consent": {
    "type": "outcome",
    "tier": "required",
    "title": "Art. 6(1)(a) — Consent",
    "summary": "Consent must be freely given, specific, informed, and unambiguous, by a clear affirmative action (Art. 4(11)). CJEU C-673/17 Planet49 confirmed: pre-ticked boxes are invalid, active behavior is required. Granular options for different purposes are required — that flows from the 'specific' element of Art. 4(11) (see Recitals 32 and 43); Art. 7(2) separately requires a consent request in a written declaration to be clearly distinguishable from other matters. Withdrawal must be as easy as giving (Art. 7(3)). For dominant platforms, dominance is a key factor in whether consent was freely given — Meta Platforms (C-252/21), an EU authority that is persuasive only in the UK.",
    "actions": [
     "UI: separate, granular, unticked checkboxes per purpose — no bundling (Planet49 (C-673/17); granularity derives from Art. 4(11) 'specific' and Recitals 32/43)",
     "Document who consented, when, and what they were told (Art. 7(1))",
     "Provide a withdrawal mechanism as easy as the consent mechanism — and surface it",
     "Conditioning a contract on consent to processing that isn't necessary for that contract will normally make the consent not freely given (Art. 7(4); EDPB Guidelines 05/2020 treat it as a strong presumption of invalidity) — avoid it",
     "Child consent age varies: EU default is 16, but Member States may set any age from 13 to 16 (Art. 8(1)) — check the age in each Member State where the service is offered (e.g. 13 in IE/SE/DK, 14 in ES/AT, 15 in FR); the UK is 13 (DPA 2018 s. 9). Below the applicable age, get parental authorization",
     "Art. 17(1)(b): withdrawing consent triggers erasure if no other basis",
     "Art. 20 portability applies",
     "Special category data: also need Art. 9(2)(a) explicit consent",
     "Cookie banners (EU/EEA): 'reject' must be as easy as 'accept' — CNIL (the French regulator) fined Google €150m in total over banner design, and the EDPB Cookie Banner Taskforce endorses the same standard",
     "Cookie banners (UK): the rule is reg. 6 of the UK's Privacy and Electronic Communications Regulations (PECR), enforced by the ICO, which has taken a similar 'reject all' position. DUAA 2025 amends PECR to permit specified low-risk cookies without consent (e.g. first-party analytics and appearance/functionality cookies, subject to information and opt-out conditions) and raises PECR fines to UK GDPR levels (up to £17.5m/4%) — verify commencement status and current ICO guidance"
    ],
    "cites": [
     "gdpr-art-6",
     "gdpr-art-4",
     "gdpr-art-8",
     "gdpr-art-9",
     "gdpr-art-17",
     "gdpr-art-20",
     "case-planet49",
     "case-meta-bka",
     "case-cnil-google-cookies",
     "uk-dpa-2018-s9",
     "uk-pecr-reg-6"
    ]
   },
